[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c59ab02-98d7-5392-bd01-7fe7417fcf03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282081} {:answer/id #uuid "3339aeb4-434f-5e0f-867c-918203782954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282148} {:answer/id #uuid "78d6e74d-1c0c-5fc8-aead-30a72958bb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282722} {:answer/id #uuid "97f6c360-16d7-53df-aca5-caeecf742ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283003} {:answer/id #uuid "a8753cd7-f0ae-5881-8198-9121d7715114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c59ab02-98d7-5392-bd01-7fe7417fcf03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282081} :answer-field/id #uuid "55b1a70b-173c-5e95-ad82-ddcd9c006883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "246a47ae-6c6b-5e78-8596-377d166e18fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-163680" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $8\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 1$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ when $t=1\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2={1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{1}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)={2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =81-16 \\\\  & =65\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c248d8-6b31-55df-90dc-c38dca6d9d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282166} {:answer/id #uuid "8204ddb8-cadf-55f1-8f7e-df954cc1809b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282806} {:answer/id #uuid "d61dbd69-68bc-5e04-913d-48cdcc5d5273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283551} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbcffb2-3c48-5a44-baa0-c1276cbbb8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283760} {:answer/id #uuid "fed9d1cf-f210-5139-bc5c-11052ee89353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35c248d8-6b31-55df-90dc-c38dca6d9d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282166} :answer-field/id #uuid "6180ffa8-3a86-5267-96ab-9659265441d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ae927bc-bcdc-5e2c-8e7d-9c28c8a41e0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163708" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(1+{t}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=1\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (1+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=0+\\frac{1}{3}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(1)=1+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\frac{16}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3566336a-5888-5f34-9857-34a10821debc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282163} {:answer/id #uuid "3e412ad6-1794-570f-ae80-6d4f0856759e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282231} {:answer/id #uuid "856b7b4b-2869-583d-992c-856b97da7887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282841} {:answer/id #uuid "acdefad4-062f-5397-94c0-1e20952ffdb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283187} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9a8ad4-ea15-5b73-937e-1a97b80f1cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e412ad6-1794-570f-ae80-6d4f0856759e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282231} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0639efc-1c73-54b7-b668-9bbd9efdc629" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a709c2cf-ccd1-5398-9c64-aedbfd0bbb7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163712" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $2{e}^{-t}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =-2{e}^{-t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=-2{e}^{-0}+C\\,⟹\\,C=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2-2{e}^{-t}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=1\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {(1)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bd2735b-7309-5cc2-aac7-6bdb82098c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465525} {:answer/id #uuid "63bba5f3-2bf2-52fb-8ebe-f9a90bfd3e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63bba5f3-2bf2-52fb-8ebe-f9a90bfd3e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465526} :answer-field/id #uuid "62291f9f-4657-5e75-859c-7df99d0d5361" :answer-field/key "$(\\pi ,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466606} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de3b1313-5c20-5038-a4d8-28551dcef349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465527} {:answer/id #uuid "093bcad8-2d30-54e0-9a4b-8040346d8fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de3b1313-5c20-5038-a4d8-28551dcef349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465527} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df5c487-ae24-53f7-907d-64c5902e3a4f" :answer-field/key "$(\\pi ,-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4aa774c-5a9f-5b63-8918-0557d528a6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465529} {:answer/id #uuid "00498319-9288-5435-aa6b-b38bb3c21b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00498319-9288-5435-aa6b-b38bb3c21b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465530} :answer-field/id #uuid "db4e3201-6ef4-5c64-9c7b-37b6045ee210" :answer-field/key "$(0,-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ce7de24-db57-5ead-8c61-93889fcd8ed1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163812" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(0,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(\\pi ,1)$; $(0,-1)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b05e796-e00c-5de2-9ed1-30703c297087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465531} {:answer/id #uuid "e130c91d-7b17-52a3-bbbe-7767095f3a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b05e796-e00c-5de2-9ed1-30703c297087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465531} :answer-field/id #uuid "1391c79c-130f-5328-917d-79dbf6fe63ec" :answer-field/key "$(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466609} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea44c227-3043-5844-9d11-64a4526e8f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465533} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1d78a3-f02f-531f-84d8-088e936f9199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea44c227-3043-5844-9d11-64a4526e8f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465533} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a5c2786-4ef5-5ec0-8f4d-c449aa97de49" :answer-field/key "$(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466610} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "604a2af8-96eb-5f23-87ff-0df87135405b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465535} {:answer/id #uuid "2598aec1-6d6c-5cb8-a449-91508e4cf00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "604a2af8-96eb-5f23-87ff-0df87135405b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465535} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcee9c7c-2aa1-5f34-a629-c4b67e147f65" :answer-field/key "$(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8eeb45f2-bc46-5681-a60d-c808242e288d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164138" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))}{{(1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)}=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement “$(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$” is false. There is no critical point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$ Therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement “$(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$” is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c77f023-69c1-596d-bd2e-ad10183ef818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282083} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4a183a-7998-57a9-b6eb-1efc1e630870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282923} {:answer/id #uuid "c156b025-2f46-52a2-a5ef-a2f486761b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283368} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f53819-035c-598d-b7dd-9bd49baaffd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283445} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e07c0b-1365-5297-87b8-d06c4846c271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c77f023-69c1-596d-bd2e-ad10183ef818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282083} :answer-field/id #uuid "434f4bd9-ebd6-5ba7-926f-d726bdc97fcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3b63dd6-0039-55fc-b091-4b9b21e853da" :question/math-academy-id "q-164139" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-3\\le y\\le 3},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 6=12\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{(\\frac{6}{5})}{12}=\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{-1+1}{2},\\frac{-3+3}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSince the center of mass is at the origin, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=-3}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{x}^{2}+18\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{[2{x}^{3}+18x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(4+36) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 40 \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =4+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =4+6 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b5e34a-6356-5793-9c72-8e26e8c9de20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281774} {:answer/id #uuid "6e011689-2b31-5709-b2da-6cd39c40793d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282632} {:answer/id #uuid "be228fb5-e5ad-5692-9acc-70c76e9e3b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283339} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c95c4d-79f6-524f-9499-a1331b472c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283373} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a5dd12-dce9-5617-9ee7-c8e2fc5f5b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2a5dd12-dce9-5617-9ee7-c8e2fc5f5b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283666} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c7a6df0-a101-5a75-be84-cfcbd906e840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a041688-e59e-573f-8208-e1c91f1707b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-164140" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=4\\cdot 2=8\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{-2+2}{2},\\frac{-1+1}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSince the center of mass is at the origin, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}x]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{32}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{40}{3}) \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =5+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =5+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cf303d-bbd6-5a0a-b641-f24761a186cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282031} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9af66e-e8ab-5330-970d-d985ef0bfcf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\sqrt{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282221} {:answer/id #uuid "8147e6af-79cc-5b38-a020-202878bc29bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282801} {:answer/id #uuid "dd297c6f-7df7-5f86-b8b6-ab58bbcbca30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283617} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b9a4ed-b340-577f-8ff3-b6abde0a73e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\sqrt{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27cf303d-bbd6-5a0a-b641-f24761a186cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282031} :answer-field/id #uuid "865a28e7-3257-5dae-a154-35f7414ee83c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04eeeae4-c27a-580c-95e7-3112d95f585b" :question/math-academy-id "q-16436" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{2},\\,h=-1,\\,k=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-1) & =\\pm (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}})(x-(-1)) \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "502cdb00-7f92-5a6a-b96d-88e4e110e0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282377} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a0ab08-d788-57a0-a416-69f93c8ee6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{t}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283108} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a134fd-43a9-5c42-8f24-4cd274dcd5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283158} {:answer/id #uuid "c70ff1fd-4dd2-52a0-8596-dd8776bb3be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283420} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3fca57-433a-598a-859d-18a3b5819d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9a134fd-43a9-5c42-8f24-4cd274dcd5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283158} :answer-field/id #uuid "b489844b-12d3-5339-a533-de1c265fdc8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ddc00eac-51aa-5d12-b01c-079fe9ee6ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-165244" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2t\\,j-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}t)}^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${t}^{2}+1>0$ for any real value $t.$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2t\\,j-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}\\,k,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+4{t}^{2}+2{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f0d5351-242d-5389-90ee-7918b4c02dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282784} {:answer/id #uuid "a16e197f-4260-5bec-a4cc-2a14f6875d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283096} {:answer/id #uuid "f25e1358-743c-5351-bef3-b379d33ee2f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283804} {:answer/id #uuid "f731f615-1ada-5e7e-8756-d36531142be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283850} {:answer/id #uuid "f84a0635-831a-5890-a477-af783dba7ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f25e1358-743c-5351-bef3-b379d33ee2f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283804} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5c0e7a3-af0b-5d46-9d00-12f81865040e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e766e306-bf27-540a-8382-0386b59b504f" :question/math-academy-id "q-165297" :question/problem "The point $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fbb49e3-e06a-56a0-aa65-1f58ccd5aa9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281974} {:answer/id #uuid "4df3b04a-46f6-50d6-91dc-4dedd3e3aa42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282362} {:answer/id #uuid "535bf8f7-77b7-5a67-8f4e-45e9966cd578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282401} {:answer/id #uuid "6c778d14-6ca9-529f-8b10-e6b5bdd36a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{11\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282619} {:answer/id #uuid "8d49ca44-b125-5b62-b216-7a8559f23fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "535bf8f7-77b7-5a67-8f4e-45e9966cd578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282401} :answer-field/id #uuid "711cae31-9076-573f-9f7d-c2f1c10a546f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17b3eea6-c61f-5eb4-a4fa-c888e2a45ea7" :question/math-academy-id "q-165299" :question/problem "The point $K$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},-1).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $K?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$  lies in the third quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we add $\\pi $ to the reference angle to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & ={\\theta }_{R}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $K$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec9ea01-bfeb-5dfa-bb0d-e8dda89e8cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281819} {:answer/id #uuid "2f167cc3-d0d4-5732-aea7-9737587d5446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282107} {:answer/id #uuid "a46851a8-a830-5300-ba0a-c94605be1ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283122} {:answer/id #uuid "a598ce3d-2796-5960-afd3-75d843fcccbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283132} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c751ad-dde6-5f48-96a4-ca006c0a37ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a598ce3d-2796-5960-afd3-75d843fcccbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283132} :answer-field/id #uuid "f183aa4f-94f5-53a7-8fa6-3240e8f8d306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47feb8ed-9de6-5cab-9530-5d8b3448a054" :question/math-academy-id "q-165413" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$, where $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab50b4da-933b-580c-90e3-d57fe918ca3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283174} {:answer/id #uuid "e0621d5d-1546-515b-b160-26c36601cee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283647} {:answer/id #uuid "eac1d727-6b15-5ede-a524-9f9b5cca59df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283741} {:answer/id #uuid "f038538b-7314-51bc-a23b-a2dc236cb725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283787} {:answer/id #uuid "f848449c-f299-591e-af24-cf140841f848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0621d5d-1546-515b-b160-26c36601cee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283647} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1857575-157f-5eb5-ba46-57df0567255a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e623a86a-8a2d-5ca1-949a-2b47126336d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-165414" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )=-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f6ddc6d-1c74-5907-9cb6-ae212f1a6518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282238} {:answer/id #uuid "72b3e334-a7c2-5074-ba45-c4f71434e66b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282671} {:answer/id #uuid "87f1206a-e8d5-5db1-ac30-dabeecd39aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282860} {:answer/id #uuid "d79038b8-ba82-5b1c-833e-0f05ff9d59e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283567} {:answer/id #uuid "f174500f-c418-5f3c-9b26-2537cef06bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f174500f-c418-5f3c-9b26-2537cef06bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283795} :answer-field/id #uuid "d883c20d-71fd-50f1-aa4d-734f491cce5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd1afd08-8a39-5c55-a110-fbcb1f5435e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-165434" :question/problem "![](images/64/6450376de8f9f4c0c8f5b414d95de67839664eda4f2056376160caddef47ba1f.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(\\frac{9}{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(\\frac{9}{2},1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4p(x-\\frac{9}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(4,0),$ we substitute $x=4$ and $y=0$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-1)^{2} & =4p(4-\\frac{9}{2}) \\\\ 1 & =4p(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2}=-2(x-\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =(0+9) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-2,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-2$ and $4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2432ea9b-d21e-5a51-8b4f-b55efb6bd9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282007} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cbd424-f3a1-5a84-9361-9c96b4e6b238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283363} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a95d55-7f7d-5b96-9047-fa934daf6737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283384} {:answer/id #uuid "d086b4fb-f27d-5ee6-8b86-c8312d6c931f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283508} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eef64f-270a-5804-b5e7-c47f798b2c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2432ea9b-d21e-5a51-8b4f-b55efb6bd9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282007} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a3e1d9-5970-53e9-bf11-467d12c1c3c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7bdbdc9-a156-56fb-bd3a-e5d66cea5150" :question/math-academy-id "q-165971" :question/problem "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5x-2}+1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5(0)-2}+1 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-2}+1 \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822b788d-d18d-56b6-8877-0b6d0fbc1127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}x+(y{e}^{z}-x{e}^{-y})\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282808} {:answer/id #uuid "c09b6cd4-0a62-5b3c-96ee-63ba9cd62535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x-x{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283362} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c83c4d-1cf4-5c68-9dce-1a5e5fa203ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}x+(y{e}^{z}+x{e}^{-y})\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283731} {:answer/id #uuid "eb38ebfb-5798-57f9-a863-de39e43e8962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}x-{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}y+y{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283744} {:answer/id #uuid "f17562cb-bf81-56aa-b3d2-47f3ff64fbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}x+({e}^{z}-x{e}^{-y})\\,\\text{d}y+y{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f17562cb-bf81-56aa-b3d2-47f3ff64fbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}x+({e}^{z}-x{e}^{-y})\\,\\text{d}y+y{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283796} :answer-field/id #uuid "87ffaad9-a455-5a60-aec1-29dc6ccdcef0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bb0fc77-ef03-5d09-9484-1c0a41da751d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167153" :question/problem "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{-y}+y{e}^{z}.$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{e}^{-y}]\\,\\text{d}x+[-x{e}^{-y}+{e}^{z}]\\,\\text{d}y+[y{e}^{z}]\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}x+({e}^{z}-x{e}^{-y})\\,\\text{d}y+y{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4828acff-c15d-5d8b-9aef-ef355a0e567c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282298} {:answer/id #uuid "488bc018-0350-58c4-baa3-b73fb7849aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282303} {:answer/id #uuid "5b56108c-83c5-51b0-97bc-ef1c90c49d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282464} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca7aaf3-036b-5d3c-81a2-2d24c2990bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282907} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1e2841-ac00-5d17-89b8-d50f0b0fa21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4828acff-c15d-5d8b-9aef-ef355a0e567c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282298} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc6259cc-71be-5ab6-b921-84982c5929c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d3c9249-0c86-56ad-b2f3-cb8e23eff378" :question/math-academy-id "q-167166" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{2y-z},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(-1,2,3)$ to $(1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}x+2x{e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}y-x{e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(-1,2,3),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(-1,2,3) & ={e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot {e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}y-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =e\\,\\text{d}x-2e\\,\\text{d}y+e\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(-1+Δx,2+Δy,3+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈e\\,Δx-2e\\,Δy+e\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(-1,2,3)$ to $(1,0,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-(-1)=2, \\\\ Δy & =0-2=-2 \\\\ Δz & =1-3=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈e\\,Δx-2e\\,Δy+e\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈e\\cdot (2)-2e\\cdot (-2)+e\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & ≈4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $4e.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6165f20f-a911-5f52-b154-c9b7146be783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282518} {:answer/id #uuid "7c25c165-90a1-52b3-950c-0524d0a78d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282758} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc9fa5-06d8-5791-9f28-2f5da3679ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283452} {:answer/id #uuid "d1675874-99c7-53bb-a75e-8153ff7cfa34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283517} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5c20cb-3aaf-53d0-987f-6298d27bfd52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc9fa5-06d8-5791-9f28-2f5da3679ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283452} :answer-field/id #uuid "8707a5fd-f08d-52ad-bc81-12c73efa5a28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ec3ee1a-9141-5963-a032-74283a58c8bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-167168" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz),$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y+\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,1,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,1,1) & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+1\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,1+Δy,1+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈Δx+Δy+Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-1=1, \\\\ Δy & =3-1=2 \\\\ Δz & =4-1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈Δx+Δy+Δz \\\\  & ≈1+2+3 \\\\  & ≈6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5152f4ba-9807-5034-82d9-7afabc4856aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282388} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd83828-03a9-5df9-ad60-8d9dfa74963f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282755} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba6475b-7aa4-50c8-8d58-f5e9707f340f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283048} {:answer/id #uuid "9ccbfa59-804e-58a4-be45-da1121b77a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283056} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6928cb-ddf2-503d-a13e-222221fd3d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ba6475b-7aa4-50c8-8d58-f5e9707f340f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283048} :answer-field/id #uuid "61486874-98c1-5a08-a0dd-be97962e4d20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "862d8f4a-ef1a-5220-a521-4d8cdae9c576" :question/math-academy-id "q-167170" :question/problem "Estimate $\\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}$ at the point $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1}=f(0.2,0.1),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(0.2,0.1).$\n\nMoving from $(0,0)$ to $(0.2,0.1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0.2-0=0.2, \\\\ Δy & =0.1-0=0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)+\\frac{1}{2}(0.1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.05 \\\\  & =0.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.15.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.2,0.1) & ≈f(0,0)+0.15 \\\\ \\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1} & ≈\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}+0.15 \\\\  & =1+0.15 \\\\  & =1.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284581 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e9f4654-e89d-5d9e-a79c-0bb5f857defd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.521" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282101} {:answer/id #uuid "85c51f51-7216-584b-b827-69d84ec4f79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282842} {:answer/id #uuid "beb360a4-ee13-5f31-9c0a-e76bcd78e6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.359" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283346} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d331ae-1ad5-5557-89ef-e15bbaae3a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.482" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283667} {:answer/id #uuid "e74c9b4d-a866-5c53-8a6c-93a7e66b72ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.274" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "beb360a4-ee13-5f31-9c0a-e76bcd78e6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.359" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283346} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff6cf4f-69f4-50e0-b11a-e6431b91e83f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9eb149a3-bdcc-552e-a682-ced670cd4013" :question/math-academy-id "q-167171" :question/problem "Estimate $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)$ at the point $({\\pi }^{2},0).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $({\\pi }^{2},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x-\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,Δx-\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)=f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $({\\pi }^{2},0)$ to $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)-{\\pi }^{2}=1, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈(-\\frac{1}{2\\pi })\\cdot (1)-1\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }-0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $-0.359.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2) & ≈f({\\pi }^{2},0)-0.359 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2) & ≈\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}+0)-0.359 \\\\  & =0-0.359 \\\\  & =-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc34e3d-7f5a-5410-9872-37c1fdc40860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282086} {:answer/id #uuid "4f619c4c-928e-5b2c-a2f6-9650ca210e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282370} {:answer/id #uuid "726c1b73-a586-5d60-b744-3f50a3ac17d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282669} {:answer/id #uuid "c39485e4-e8d5-5bd1-a275-66e3b3650f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283393} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd21f5f-8b88-5095-9dca-4fe1f18d7abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c39485e4-e8d5-5bd1-a275-66e3b3650f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283393} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0bec54-298c-5799-a502-9235e4f8885b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f5ee281-743c-52e5-8ba6-b17dfb13fd85" :question/math-academy-id "q-167338" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec6deda5b2400258af6ad455cdfa0072deb754391feb4d916082568c248d95ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,7)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "439aee45-933e-5525-9345-5b17029a63c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282266} {:answer/id #uuid "582ea5c1-1e57-5176-9167-972093895fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282440} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb3e4fe-ec5b-5339-9652-4a5cb0a96a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282782} {:answer/id #uuid "848c5f97-904d-5825-a1c2-c3a1c330760f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282831} {:answer/id #uuid "e9802ab6-19b7-5148-b0e2-623781519ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "848c5f97-904d-5825-a1c2-c3a1c330760f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282831} :answer-field/id #uuid "353e1da0-d350-56e5-9994-3f64a6bca475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2df41f1-db14-5e76-bbfb-6cc0c8cd31a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-167497" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\pi $ given that $∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=\\frac{t}{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j) \\\\  & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\pi ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (-1)+0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "945b9c04-6a85-5d2f-aac7-20fb300b68cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282964} {:answer/id #uuid "a0759ec4-44c2-5df4-8049-d2141efec4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283086} {:answer/id #uuid "cefe4dd8-87f4-5862-998f-f2f8be85aafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283496} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5f7e03-4f52-5b2c-83bb-c9656091962b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283757} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bb78c7-3837-59e6-b577-9cf297b1d8d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec5f7e03-4f52-5b2c-83bb-c9656091962b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283757} :answer-field/id #uuid "d53902b6-01db-5851-b51a-31934c273c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b00d973d-5785-5a66-98c1-0a412869e903" :question/math-academy-id "q-167499" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t}⟩$ at the point $t=9.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y},-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=\\sqrt{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(9))] & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263de2e3-27ae-52e2-8582-4dfb39ba6ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282023} {:answer/id #uuid "75614e75-3308-50a7-a253-f1f9b76e3296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2t+1}}\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282694} {:answer/id #uuid "76433ca0-b7f8-577e-a495-c0ae272242e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282701} {:answer/id #uuid "89172764-a6f4-5672-8136-d35db577b74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t(2t+1)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282872} {:answer/id #uuid "8caffd9e-930e-5e4b-b97e-bcbc15c29c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76433ca0-b7f8-577e-a495-c0ae272242e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282701} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee764035-aa19-5d16-9448-a4b9dd4171f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bb4ae8a-0b75-5e62-bc9b-0900b739960b" :question/math-academy-id "q-167608" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $0\\le t\\le 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2tj \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{4y+1}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1})\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48320d50-11dc-5c25-b211-1a6293887c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282299} {:answer/id #uuid "667ecc69-8180-56b1-a381-85cc0e7c7286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282577} {:answer/id #uuid "7b621e50-9338-5847-b8c4-30f5e5589d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282748} {:answer/id #uuid "90c1007a-4679-560a-84f7-593c87ce0e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282941} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac19e51-1f63-5f51-98b2-8211bafdd295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48320d50-11dc-5c25-b211-1a6293887c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282299} :answer-field/id #uuid "69141054-77da-5506-94da-51c42eb49c54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72644873-b2ba-57a8-bd4c-d5dc5e8e5941" :question/math-academy-id "q-167609" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,1+t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨t,\\,1+t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$    \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t+(1+t)=2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2t+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{[{t}^{2}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1+1-0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c74ebde-4483-557d-bc7f-96d2eae35d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281803} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1f1600-c667-5053-87bf-a32e6404cd47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282216} {:answer/id #uuid "8298ffad-58f2-5c03-8c84-d9f00f0346bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7(2\\sqrt{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282818} {:answer/id #uuid "c84c1cb8-787a-5d55-a741-7cdf8092bf77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7(\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283442} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bd9128-7071-5f60-80ca-f9fcc77741a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c1f1600-c667-5053-87bf-a32e6404cd47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282216} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c701af0-373b-5ef2-ba21-ba5e8d9c684f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2dda2ab-f3d6-5a31-a33f-5b15f55a34c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167612" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=3t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j\\,$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=3t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=3t$ and $y={t}^{3}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t)^{3}+{t}^{3} \\\\  & =27{t}^{3}+{t}^{3} \\\\  & =28{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}28{t}^{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}84{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{4},$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}84{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}21\\cdot {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =21\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =14({2}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10206875-a5c4-5022-9ee9-43b3a21b42b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281836} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd849f2-6a5c-5409-a292-f59a3dc7a36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282215} {:answer/id #uuid "4544a980-713e-5e34-893c-6b08076a1997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282275} {:answer/id #uuid "7f63b4af-1a8d-5157-bbb0-dc8287c37b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282786} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a5f08d-e51f-5026-8bb2-63d5b7342f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f63b4af-1a8d-5157-bbb0-dc8287c37b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282786} :answer-field/id #uuid "b92feada-828f-5f43-8105-e8387a4f1f6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2907fc18-ef26-5825-bc1f-b4cb4bdbf7cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-167621" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$  \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-16$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-16 \\\\  & =16(2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =32[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0] \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfabe82-a3a8-543e-850f-9d1fed597483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}+7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281809} {:answer/id #uuid "1363d5a4-c6f8-5339-80e9-71cac29d8c31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3x}{25{x}^{3}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281875} {:answer/id #uuid "5770169f-b317-5cdf-ab13-67b1aac4ff76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{12-4{x}^{5}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282432} {:answer/id #uuid "62403495-1a5a-5db7-9f20-44a808e3aec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt[3]{9-{x}^{5}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282527} {:answer/id #uuid "f9029b6d-5807-5f23-8ab3-0868cdbe97e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{49-9{x}^{5}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9029b6d-5807-5f23-8ab3-0868cdbe97e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{49-9{x}^{5}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283868} :answer-field/id #uuid "7222ea84-407e-559e-8445-ad062fd87ba4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66ed24c2-b0c7-5f5d-a2d3-b464d89d1f07" :question/math-academy-id "q-168138" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}=3{y}^{4}+2xy,\\,y(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{6}{x}\\,z=-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{-3}.$*" :question/worked-solution "The substitution $z={y}^{-3}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{6}{x}z=-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{6}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 6/x\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={x}^{6}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{6}{x}\\,z) & ={x}^{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {x}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+6{x}^{5}z & =-9{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{x}^{6}z] & =-9{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{x}^{6}z]\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -9{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{6}z & =-\\frac{9}{5}{x}^{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-9{x}^{5}}{5{x}^{6}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5{C}_{1}.$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-3}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-3} & =\\frac{C-9{x}^{5}}{5{x}^{6}} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{C-9{x}^{5}} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{C-9{x}^{5}}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition  $y(1)=\\frac{1}{2}$ we determine $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5}{C-9}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & =\\frac{5}{C-9} \\\\ C-9 & =40 \\\\ C & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{49-9{x}^{5}}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30ff9de6-82b9-57fd-8352-549a13be4d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,\\text{d}a+9\\,\\text{d}b+64\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282128} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae0312d-ecd7-57dc-9d88-15ff8ec29a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{d}a+24\\,\\text{d}b+48\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282330} {:answer/id #uuid "55b45edc-b405-5927-afc4-f43852758102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{d}a+48\\,\\text{d}b+18\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282415} {:answer/id #uuid "8abb4554-2c3b-5729-8dbb-6cd315e8c4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{d}a+3\\,\\text{d}b+9\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282885} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a31cef-0e81-5be3-8ef5-5772fcc10f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{d}a+24\\,\\text{d}b+9\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55b45edc-b405-5927-afc4-f43852758102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{d}a+48\\,\\text{d}b+18\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282415} :answer-field/id #uuid "8563a43e-521b-5142-9d50-25857d86ed4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56bdc966-10cb-53e1-a679-ccd338b6c565" :question/math-academy-id "q-168150" :question/problem "Given a rectangular solid, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ of the rectangular solid if the lengths of the sides are $a=6,$ $b=3,$ and $c=8.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the rectangular solid is $V=abc.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(a,b,c) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂a})\\,\\text{d}a+(\\frac{∂V}{∂b})\\,\\text{d}b+(\\frac{∂V}{∂c})\\,\\text{d}c \\\\  & =bc\\,\\text{d}a+ac\\,\\text{d}b+ab\\,\\text{d}c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=6,$ $b=3,$ and $c=8,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(6,3,8) & =3\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}a+6\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}b+6\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}c \\\\  & =24\\,\\text{d}a+48\\,\\text{d}b+18\\,\\text{d}c.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bdc068e-8c3f-5185-8c24-fdc3cd60d793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},4,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281801} {:answer/id #uuid "62f05e6b-44fd-53bc-ab6d-4879faa39fac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},-2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282538} {:answer/id #uuid "63bd9582-2b65-5d17-ab6a-c759c54d1865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282548} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c826f3-22b5-5270-96c6-5f2bb7795bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},-5,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283294} {:answer/id #uuid "c844a75f-f459-5dee-b756-15fd8a8cbad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63bd9582-2b65-5d17-ab6a-c759c54d1865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282548} :answer-field/id #uuid "21dab653-f4e8-5ddf-9381-f42d4fdfb37e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c7e0dbe-72ee-5a82-9a45-e2d81377354c" :question/math-academy-id "q-168896" :question/problem "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(8,\\frac{\\pi }{6},-5).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198b8e84-f193-531e-9d0d-8ec40e368ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{5},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281922} {:answer/id #uuid "1cced431-2ae3-55b2-96db-c772a52758f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281950} {:answer/id #uuid "360bb19a-a68d-594f-ac04-f07e75deb3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282168} {:answer/id #uuid "c7107144-3c77-5cc1-8728-0f09146fc616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283421} {:answer/id #uuid "dba994c1-93af-5ece-bb12-4d9df3c8d3d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "360bb19a-a68d-594f-ac04-f07e75deb3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282168} :answer-field/id #uuid "11d48e34-c312-5a42-b107-376386ecb8c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab2ff8e7-fadd-5382-9958-646985c495b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-16951" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(5,5).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot {5}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the point $P$ lies in the first quadrant, we can determine $\\theta $ by taking the arctangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(5\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06556ef1-f7c9-50f6-9c43-5e14baf287ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281744} {:answer/id #uuid "3650f7a1-05f1-5ec8-bfa8-7de358994a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282171} {:answer/id #uuid "561e6a5f-5878-5845-a87a-2bfe8a0ddaa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282419} {:answer/id #uuid "e35a4060-3854-5a02-a79e-8cb8c152f7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283672} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3b59cc-ce86-5661-b860-dc0f8d9d9699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e35a4060-3854-5a02-a79e-8cb8c152f7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283672} :answer-field/id #uuid "dca2fc78-f40e-5eab-b2b8-35ed04e2a6c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26491941-c22b-5ecf-8cb8-b5c09aa24db5" :question/math-academy-id "q-16953" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the point $P$ lies in the first quadrant, we can determine $\\theta $ by taking the arctangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145ea3ab-ba48-51a3-9cb0-36a8e90fa34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281881} {:answer/id #uuid "165ea686-7c2e-59b0-b9aa-d3a6d73b845e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{y}^{2}-20{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281897} {:answer/id #uuid "200c8291-90d1-5064-a4f4-1ca33a402b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2}-4{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281978} {:answer/id #uuid "a17fe031-7bef-5a73-afea-0455026b93e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}-4{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283097} {:answer/id #uuid "b2433c9a-0daf-54ae-bec2-f04286cf16d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9{y}^{2}-20{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2433c9a-0daf-54ae-bec2-f04286cf16d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9{y}^{2}-20{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283234} :answer-field/id #uuid "b58dff51-483d-547c-8b8a-37b3aca06f12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "570f79f9-b528-59df-a2a6-a750762e3979" :question/math-academy-id "q-170132" :question/problem "If $x=3{y}^{3}-4{y}^{5},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3{y}^{3}-4{y}^{5}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3})-4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{5}) \\\\  & =9{y}^{2}-20{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{9{y}^{2}-20{y}^{4}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e89892f-6177-5da8-ba8f-f6ad4938f860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281816} {:answer/id #uuid "12b70b84-7322-57f9-a48d-b97394706f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281867} {:answer/id #uuid "8ced86e9-fb84-576d-be70-7a5432a09c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282911} {:answer/id #uuid "cd423368-a8a3-578c-8fba-929065592761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283482} {:answer/id #uuid "f43a7ca7-597b-5512-aa7c-ee8e4811dc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd423368-a8a3-578c-8fba-929065592761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283482} :answer-field/id #uuid "012d375b-46c2-5142-9bad-9eca1ad9eaec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0eb61aef-5332-5039-a2f0-c31d2273ab77" :question/math-academy-id "q-170140" :question/problem "If $x=y-2{y}^{3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y-2{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =1-6{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1-6{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(1,-1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(1,-1)} & =\\frac{1}{1-6(-1)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42870b8a-6811-57e2-a36a-d2c4873163b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=16x-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282257} {:answer/id #uuid "4c60e32d-c9e7-591c-b44e-871777f79e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{16}-\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282342} {:answer/id #uuid "563209de-e3ba-5eb3-aeac-ce115fd5d616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=16x+31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282421} {:answer/id #uuid "6d76402d-6c5f-5efd-830d-ec6c00b0290e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{16}-\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282626} {:answer/id #uuid "de9b6704-8f4a-51f5-9f16-2568dbe38ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{16}+\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d76402d-6c5f-5efd-830d-ec6c00b0290e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{16}-\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282626} :answer-field/id #uuid "e01f8527-80c3-523b-ae6b-b9ef56fe0470" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f67b5768-4734-533a-a97b-6ff6cd26cf82" :question/math-academy-id "q-170188" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=5{y}^{5}-3{y}^{3},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(-2,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(5{y}^{5}-3{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{5})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (5{y}^{4})-3\\cdot (3{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =25{y}^{4}-9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{25{y}^{4}-9{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(-2,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-2,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25(-1)^{4}-9(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =\\frac{1}{16}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{1}{16}(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{16}+\\frac{2}{16}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{16}+\\frac{1}{8}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{16}-\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43180867-e54c-5b2d-baa1-cf9a1cbe0b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{33}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282261} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9f3558-657f-579f-8054-76a165cec2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{10}+\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282607} {:answer/id #uuid "772d57cb-a077-5382-a7cf-063edbf390c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282709} {:answer/id #uuid "b61872c4-87ff-5f2f-a588-6b1bb2670cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283270} {:answer/id #uuid "bf08dc7d-e699-5a3a-8693-a33d5378ca66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-10x+44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b61872c4-87ff-5f2f-a588-6b1bb2670cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283270} :answer-field/id #uuid "5645007e-953e-5e1b-8348-3a7a0c4acd9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5409375b-690a-557c-9e51-1e26c492e709" :question/math-academy-id "q-170189" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=y+\\frac{3}{{y}^{3}},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+\\frac{3}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-3}) \\\\  & =1+3\\cdot (-3{y}^{-4}) \\\\  & =1-9{y}^{-4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{{y}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{4}-9}{{y}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{y}^{4}-9}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{4}}{{1}^{4}-9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05dc1d0c-6562-5063-8b6a-fb2761a7610a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281740} {:answer/id #uuid "2c23b35a-058b-5db4-9003-d4cc23104b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{20}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282078} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6123f8-d142-5770-8b49-a723c5b081c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282484} {:answer/id #uuid "815ab18e-3bdc-566d-897a-290bc02c3418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282802} {:answer/id #uuid "cccc287f-7075-53df-994c-57ed770387d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{20}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c23b35a-058b-5db4-9003-d4cc23104b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{20}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282078} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de7d9ff-8de4-57f9-8a25-279d79c35b36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "17ae717b-d689-565d-9092-d0ded31ed5c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-17019" :question/problem "Calculate the integral\n$\\int \\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (3-4x)^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=3-4x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3-4x)^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{2/3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5/3}{u}^{5/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}{u}^{5/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{20}{u}^{5/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{20}(3-4x)^{5/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{20}\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{5}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{3}{20}(3-4x)^{5/3}+C] & =-\\frac{3}{20}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(3-4x)^{5/3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{20}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3}(3-4x)^{2/3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3-4x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(3-4x)^{3/2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =(3-4x)^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e62d273-60bf-5db3-944b-c2ec80f9bd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{9}x-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282098} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb97988-f068-523c-bf95-8ec539b657eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282475} {:answer/id #uuid "943d0ec3-2bf9-5fa4-ade6-5a365d7cf644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282962} {:answer/id #uuid "da6c0b7d-2776-529c-bf98-cd4ecc08c32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283595} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e111b0-ce81-58fb-b4a0-91289945c59e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{9}{2}x-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cb97988-f068-523c-bf95-8ec539b657eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282475} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed15c158-3a37-5334-8d3c-ea6867dc131d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "750fb285-3a69-5ae0-b2fe-f98b94894525" :question/math-academy-id "q-170190" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-2{y}^{2},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(-1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/2})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-3/2}-2\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{y}^{3/2}}-4y \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-8{y}^{5/2}}{2{y}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+8{y}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{2y\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=-\\frac{2y\\sqrt{y}}{1+8{y}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(1)\\sqrt{1}}{1+8(1)^{2}\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{9}(x-(-1)) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{9}x-\\frac{2}{9}+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{7}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc5d0dd-5ad7-53e0-a487-da341a2c62a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281975} {:answer/id #uuid "42c04421-6005-5881-906e-578fd0eb9e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282258} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2c8a58-cb94-5647-8e55-be625d2465fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282612} {:answer/id #uuid "a01f9a32-ae04-515e-8c51-0d4b1caf0aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283081} {:answer/id #uuid "a82eafe8-6b8d-58bd-9fb7-c053bbe0e71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a82eafe8-6b8d-58bd-9fb7-c053bbe0e71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283152} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c7deb3a-0f80-5d69-9111-7c0d9df7a995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14d04b2f-bdf7-50aa-8934-3af1da6231ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-170191" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{{y}^{3}})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}-2{y}^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{1/2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{7/2}-4}{2{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3{y}^{7/2}-4}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{3(1)^{7/2}-4} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-2(x-2) \\\\ y & =-2x+4+1 \\\\ y & =-2x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40416ccd-9b64-5689-9d0e-17f5f4751565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6x-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282241} {:answer/id #uuid "a11133ec-85bc-51d5-ae7c-e2cb96b0b7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283091} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c23f80-893c-5073-98ff-c450d839112c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{23}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283271} {:answer/id #uuid "e82961ce-854e-507f-a418-04c390deed84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283711} {:answer/id #uuid "ffac7ffe-6d41-5fe9-bbf6-a0f3c1e1b667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffac7ffe-6d41-5fe9-bbf6-a0f3c1e1b667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283930} :answer-field/id #uuid "929d2cf9-0b56-521f-8aa6-eccabe5878d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d82342b-72a0-50b7-825a-706ef7bb48d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-170192" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}-1,$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}-1) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (2y)-2{y}^{-3}-0 \\\\  & =8y-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{4}-2}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{8{y}^{4}-2}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)^{3}}{8(1)^{4}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{1}{6}(x-4) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{2}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39e3d197-2479-5d41-9a2c-54e25e8c3918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{13}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282199} {:answer/id #uuid "4625694b-2b15-59f8-9537-f51d6e1a6e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282282} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7b75a6-c784-575c-b176-3c8b0f974993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{21}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282472} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bca605-8348-51b3-a0a9-15f002d2c63b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283543} {:answer/id #uuid "ef77ce21-26ef-50a0-98ad-4c05fa847c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4625694b-2b15-59f8-9537-f51d6e1a6e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282282} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ecc8de7-bf89-52fc-97f5-b5d695109712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ddadb84c-610f-5530-9030-0ca4688d37d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-170349" :question/problem "For the curve $y=2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2},$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{1}{2}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10110147-d53a-52b4-9412-60917bae18a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281834} {:answer/id #uuid "35ebf50d-9e51-5dd6-90ce-6b42609e1277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282167} {:answer/id #uuid "4a227723-7044-54e3-b22e-e4d9e959d300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282319} {:answer/id #uuid "59445ca6-d8f8-5abc-befc-bb2cef4921d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282448} {:answer/id #uuid "f114926f-bae0-59d7-a3c0-0d8adbad48cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a227723-7044-54e3-b22e-e4d9e959d300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282319} :answer-field/id #uuid "59d527ed-62e9-59c3-b626-0a9ba794e766" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40f36d04-c36f-53cb-8067-f12e5e811687" :question/math-academy-id "q-170350" :question/problem "![](images/06/06ea604e70390627f9e98dbea5048f27bd1f63d0b10002bbd5518c000b5125bf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{4}{3},$ hence $c=\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3} & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "392c37bf-2eff-5710-94c4-5c95cf077622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282192} {:answer/id #uuid "441bb609-4b3d-5573-abf9-666d72266682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282268} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa9f774-9dc3-5517-b49a-23477f5ef398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282884} {:answer/id #uuid "9487b2d2-a4c7-507f-90c3-935a34ee6aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282967} {:answer/id #uuid "ef011d02-08c1-5542-89a5-e726fcdc2d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "441bb609-4b3d-5573-abf9-666d72266682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282268} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e0cc21-4467-59ba-96d0-fe4d79911e7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc30ca5f-d528-5c9f-ac69-bfc015b0707a" :question/math-academy-id "q-170351" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc0e258229c74a97d61919440090dd7b2f0f4a200af987477d2be8818910a88b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{1}{4},$ hence $c=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{4} & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k+\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\frac{5}{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d3ffe79-cf43-530e-8eae-0e60fe0f23e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282225} {:answer/id #uuid "6660bdbd-1b8f-58f9-876e-d6a2b918789f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282574} {:answer/id #uuid "79d844dd-16d0-52f9-a8c5-8b32f3ac58bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282731} {:answer/id #uuid "d3dcca19-c5a2-51a6-a3a6-41318cfceb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283536} {:answer/id #uuid "e71e11ae-ae0e-5e45-ba55-9d15f63a2382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6660bdbd-1b8f-58f9-876e-d6a2b918789f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282574} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c06587-0163-510d-8601-75701f5c6a47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3aab57aa-ff99-5777-9d17-d6c1ad14589b" :question/math-academy-id "q-170486" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-8+6\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/db/db85a52dd73f7e908815632bb803c472e507d32f1636c2b4a7f6c72d131405d3.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{8} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{6}{8}) \\\\  & ≈0.643\\,50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈\\pi -0.643\\,50≈2.50\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc383aa-c6d8-5cb4-9f37-78f78398d296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282112} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcd893c-01c1-52b0-8774-2ca408dfc3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282899} {:answer/id #uuid "97b0eb56-a4d3-596c-a9c5-af16c716e2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283002} {:answer/id #uuid "e5691c8f-e6ed-5728-ace3-4968571878b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283691} {:answer/id #uuid "ef691221-3ed8-5189-8810-d04927763ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef691221-3ed8-5189-8810-d04927763ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283774} :answer-field/id #uuid "a32f94de-a65d-57b8-a1d7-93ea2655c742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "876ee8ae-f199-5a6e-ad24-4a4c31d3be35" :question/math-academy-id "q-170494" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=9-6\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1b/1bc5f38d44d9df32a7493f8d210bbc17d716d8a63316e3d730a1c4258d56fe46.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan⁡(\\frac{6}{9})=arctan⁡(\\frac{2}{3})≈0.588\\,00.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.588\\,00≈5.70\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c130348-fdf2-5cec-b6b7-e100af6ee199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ed/ed0fd77369cb0536c80bc76cdc9cf252539db844b5cb6944a5df89214ef336a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282076} {:answer/id #uuid "43e8abdb-0bbe-5780-ba00-981286ebbf0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b